"""Question -> evidence -> interpretation copy for the final 32-figure report."""
BASE='https://aithal007.github.io/DV_assignment2/'
ABSTRACT='''What do averages and snapshots hide about inequality, relationships and change over time? We study country internet indicators and daily US weather separately. Network, multivariable, temporal and spatial views connect each question to measured evidence. Broadband and internet use correlate strongly across country levels (0.831), but weakly across their changes (0.054). About 47% of measured adoption variation lies within regions. Hot-dry conditions occupy 16.27% of weighted grid-cell days, yet local runs reach 67 days. These contrasts show why group summaries, endpoints and separate frequencies need individual values, histories and shared timing for interpretation.'''
INTRO='''A national adoption rate hides infrastructure differences; a regional mean hides its member countries; a monthly weather summary hides daily events. We use linked comparisons to recover that missing detail.

**Central question: What do averages and snapshots hide about inequality, relationships and change over time?**

We ask: (1) Do similar internet-use levels conceal different telecom profiles? (2) Does overall progress imply catch-up among the same countries? (3) What do weather averages and selected dates omit about location, event timing and duration? Country data answer the first two questions; the separate weather investigation answers the third. Each result is interpreted as a descriptive pattern in the included data, with its scope stated.'''
DATA='''**Country inputs and cleaning.** Three A1 OWID/ITU CSV extracts [2] provide internet-user counts, internet-use percentages and telecom subscriptions. `scripts/preprocess_data.py` renames columns, checks source-key uniqueness and numeric types, then outer-joins the files on entity, country code and year with one-to-one validation. The join has 13,387 rows; removing 823 explicitly listed aggregate rows leaves 12,564 country-year records. Missing values remain missing: they are neither replaced with zero nor imputed. A separate code-based lookup supplies regions; unmapped entries remain Unclassified.

**Comparable cohorts and transformations.** Each task declares its complete cases: 179 countries with all four 2020 profile indicators for the network; the same 175 countries in both 2015 and 2020 for paired changes; 159 countries with all 21 annual adoption values for 2000-2020; and 184 countries with user counts and adoption for treemaps. A change is computed within country before summarizing. Internet use is a percentage of people; landline, broadband and mobile values are subscriptions per 100 people and may exceed 100. These units are retained in profiles and changes.

**Professor's guidance and network construction.** Following the professor's advice, we score every country pair, inspect the similarity distribution and filter highly similar pairs. Four indicators are z-scored separately across the 179 countries: subtract the indicator's country mean and divide by its standard deviation. Euclidean distance d gives similarity s=1/(1+d). Our team chooses the 95th percentile, P95=0.569763, as the sparse default: 797 of 15,931 pairs remain, with all 179 nodes retained. Perturbation was mentioned as an option; P95 is our team's choice. Figure 1 explains that choice and its sensitivity.

**Weather period and sampling rationale.** gridMET [1] supplies `tmmx` (daily maximum temperature), `pr` (rain), `vs` (wind speed at 10 m) and `th` (wind FROM-direction at 10 m). July-September spans peak summer into early autumn, allowing heat persistence, cooling and rain timing to be compared in one contiguous interval. The selected 2023 year includes independently documented Southwest July heat [9], making persistence substantively relevant; it is a case study, not a representative climate year. Uniform eight-date sampling, 13 days apart with both endpoints, gives reproducible seasonal coverage without the uneven gaps possible in a small random sample. It cannot ensure capture of short extremes; all 92 days support monthly/event inferences.

**Weather preparation.** Decode NetCDF packing/masks before converting source Kelvin to Celsius; supplied caches are already Celsius. Read every sixth grid cell (subsampling, not averaging), align coordinates/dates and retain the same 13,416 cells valid for all variables on all days. Cosine-latitude weights approximate area. Rain stays mm/day, speed m/s and direction degrees FROM north; quiver components point toward flow. The audit records hashes, missing counts, cohorts and sampled-to-daily checks.'''
JS='''NetworkX [3] computes three layouts; Gephi [5] is the assignment-permitted alternative and is not used here. D3 [4] provides the threshold slider, layout selection, region/isolate node filters, PCP interactions and treemap zoom/hierarchy/partition controls. These are the required working node-link interactions, not merely static layout images. Report diagrams are chart exports; complete uncropped browser captures with controls are linked through the [UI evidence gallery](https://aithal007.github.io/DV_assignment2/interactions.html). Use the dashboard or `python run_visualizations.py`; the README gives full reproduction commands.'''
REPRO='''The repository README documents exact commands for setup, preprocessing, rebuilding supplied figures into the report, regenerating results from raw country extracts and weather caches, running checks, and refreshing the live site. Source CSVs, cache metadata, preprocessing audit, figure exports and editable LaTeX are included. Use the README at the same repository revision as this report; it identifies required software, inputs, outputs and optional downloads.'''
DISCUSSION='''The country evidence answers why an improving summary cannot describe every country. Network layouts rearrange the same relationships; threshold and matrix checks reveal what a grouped picture hides. Figure 31 makes the headline problem explicit: only 71 of 454 pairs with almost equal adoption meet the full-profile cutoff. Paired paths and change PCPs recover different histories behind progress. The level-versus-gain comparison weakens the broadband association from 0.83 to 0.05, so a snapshot relationship cannot explain the ordering of gains. Fixed cohorts retain a widening absolute gap, and 47% of measured adoption variance lies within regions. Country identities and paired observations are necessary for these inferences.

Weather evidence similarly separates level, timing and coincidence. Two selected days disagree in change sign with complete months over 18.26% of the area. Rainfall concentration exposes timing concealed by monthly totals. Hot-dry frequency, longest runs and daily coverage describe different event properties; the calendar shows equal 75-day counts with 17- versus 67-day longest spells. Separate hot, dry and windy frequencies cannot recover their 2.57% joint occurrence.

Together the comparisons recover within-group differences, histories and daily order hidden by aggregation. These descriptive results concern the stated countries, grid and period; they do not establish causes, universal country types or a climate trend.'''

TAKEAWAYS={
1:'Filtering makes the complete pair network readable, but also changes connectedness. P95 keeps 797 edges and 12 isolates; the three layouts preserve those relationships. Infer similarity from scores and edges, while treating layout proximity and isolated placement as display choices.',
2:'Similar adoption progress can accompany different infrastructure histories. The contrasting country paths show that a 2020 endpoint cannot establish how a country reached it; annual paired observations are needed to distinguish those histories.',
3:'A community label hides unequal relationships among its members. The enlarged matrix contains 93 of 105 possible links, with uneven row degrees and real gaps. Inspect those pairs before treating a network community as one uniform telecom profile.',
4:'Similar internet-use gains conceal different combinations of infrastructure change. Even the selected 20-30-point gain group contains opposing landline and mobile movements. Follow each country across all four change axes before assigning one explanation to its progress.',
5:'High adoption describes several infrastructure profiles rather than one common model. Overlapping mobile values and widely differing broadband values within the same adoption band show why the headline rate must be read alongside the other indicators.',
6:'A correlation summarizes an association while hiding its exceptions and shape. The scatter cells show stronger adoption association with broadband than mobile, yet substantial variation remains. These endpoint associations do not identify the drivers of country progress.',
7:'Countries that have more broadband tend to have higher adoption, but countries gaining more broadband do not show the same ordering of adoption gains. The fall from 0.83 to 0.05 exposes why a cross-country snapshot cannot substitute for paired change.',
8:'A large internet-user count can coexist with incomplete population access. Asia holds 56.9% of included users, but rectangle size alone says nothing about adoption percentage. Read count area and country color together to distinguish scale from access.',
9:'The distribution of users across adoption levels becomes visible when the hierarchy changes. The 50-below-75% band contains 39.7% of included users, so many online users live where substantial access gaps remain. Grouping changes the question answered by the same values.',
10:'Broad progress does not imply absolute catch-up. All fixed starting groups improve, yet their low-to-high median gap widens from 16.2 to 53.8 points. Fixed membership establishes that this contrast is not created by moving countries between groups.',
11:'Regional averages leave substantial country inequality unresolved. The 47% within-region variance share and the individual dots show that a regional label cannot stand in for its members. Preserve country-level spread when interpreting an improving regional summary.',
12:'A shift in a regional median is a different quantity from a typical country gain. Asia gives 26.55 versus 19.75 points. Calculate paired changes first when the question concerns individual progress; summarizing first can change the answer.',
13:'The same final adoption level can hide different arrival times. Horizon rows preserve early, late and absent crossings of the 50% milestone. Their colors describe position relative to that reference; they do not by themselves indicate growth or decline.',
14:'Regional trajectories make broad timing differences clear while compressing member histories. The large Europe-Africa median gap does not describe every country in either region. Read these summaries with country horizons and within-region spread before generalizing.',
15:'A rising country median does not mean adoption has become uniform. Despite the rise from 2.2% to 71.4%, the 2020 middle half spans 37.5 points. Distribution width qualifies the progress visible in the central value.',
16:'Global progress combines different regional histories and changing internal spreads. A single global ridge therefore cannot represent one shared journey. Country marks are essential when small groups make a smooth density shape look more certain than the observations support.',
17:'Color mapping controls which measured differences receive visual emphasis. Linear rain color prioritizes absolute amounts, while logarithmic color separates small positive amounts. Interpret a feature through its legend and normalization before calling it a stronger weather event.',
18:'Consistent colors are necessary for comparing temperature across dates. Daily rescaling can assign the same bright color to different temperatures, concealing absolute change. Use fixed bounds for temporal comparison and daily bounds for within-date contrast.',
19:'More contour lines increase display detail without increasing data resolution. Five-degree spacing reveals more threshold crossings; filled bands emphasize broad regions. Select the representation for the reading task and infer boundaries only at the stated levels.',
20:'The monthly maps establish broad cooling while retaining its spatial pattern. The mean daily maximum falls by 4.64 degrees, but a monthly average cannot count heat episodes or their duration. Daily and frequency views supply those missing dimensions.',
21:'Two endpoint days can misrepresent change across the full interval. Their change sign disagrees with the complete-month comparison over 18.26% of the study area. Use all days when the claim concerns monthly conditions rather than two weather states.',
22:'A smooth area mean can conceal both daily episodes and simultaneous differences between places. The ribbons recover that spatial spread around the daily mean. Their width describes variation across cells, rather than statistical uncertainty about the mean.',
23:'Monthly rainfall amount cannot identify how rain arrived. Similar July-August totals conceal different five-day concentration, and September has the largest concentration share despite a lower total. Compare amount with timing, using the explicitly eligible area for each month.',
24:'Hot-day frequency reveals repeated heat that a monthly mean or one selected date cannot count. Its decline from July to September measures occurrence, while the maps retain location. Frequency still leaves the ordering of those days unresolved.',
25:'Retaining every date exposes episodes smoothed away by monthly maps. The time-longitude view locates their timing and east-west extent, but latitude averaging removes north-south detail. Return to maps before interpreting a band as a specific event location or track.',
26:'Arrow spacing and length determine which wind questions are readable. Sparse sampling clarifies directions while omitting locations; equal lengths emphasize direction and speed-scaled lengths add magnitude. Interpret the vector convention and speed colors with the chosen design.',
27:'Wind direction and strength distributions change within the three-month interval. Monthly roses expose a source-direction shift that a pooled seasonal summary could hide. Their common radial scale supports frequency comparison, while maps remain necessary to locate the winds.',
28:'Separate field summaries cannot establish that conditions coincide at the same place. The overlay aligns heat, rain and wind to reveal spatial coincidence on selected dates. Those examples motivate the complete-period overlap calculation rather than establish its frequency.',
29:'Common individual conditions need not produce common joint events. Although dry days occupy 76.65% of weighted cell-days, only 2.57% meet all three conditions together. Compute overlap at the same cell and date; separate marginal percentages cannot recover it.',
30:'Occurrence, uninterrupted duration and affected area describe different event properties. The 16.27% hot-dry frequency and 67-day local maximum cannot substitute for each other. Combine their maps, daily coverage and threshold sensitivity before characterizing the seasonal pattern.',
31:'Almost equal adoption rates can conceal substantially different infrastructure. Only 71 of 454 eligible pairs meet the full-profile cutoff. The headline percentage therefore cannot substitute for a country profile, even when two values nearly match.',
32:'Equal event counts can hide very different persistence. Two cells each have 75 hot-dry days, yet longest spells of 17 and 67 days. Preserving daily order is necessary to distinguish scattered occurrences from sustained conditions.'}

def links(n,display_number=None):
    if n==1:return [('View live demonstration',BASE+'web/network.html'),('Full UI screenshot',BASE+'interactions.html#network')]
    if n==4:return [('View live demonstration',BASE+'output/pcp_changes/index.html'),('Full UI screenshot',BASE+'interactions.html#changes')]
    if n==5:return [('View live demonstration',BASE+'web/index.html'),('Full UI screenshot',BASE+'interactions.html#pcp')]
    if n in {8,9}:return [('View live demonstration',BASE+'web/treemap.html'),('Full UI screenshot',BASE+'interactions.html#treemap')]
    if n==18:return [('View fixed-scale animation',BASE+'images/scivis/scivis_global.gif'),('View daily-scale animation',BASE+'images/scivis/scivis_local.gif')]
    if n==19:return [('View contour animation',BASE+'images/scivis/scivis_contour.gif')]
    return [('View figure and interpretation online',BASE+f'figures.html#fig{display_number or n}')]

def apply(figures,content=None):
    from report_interpretations import DETAIL
    if content is not None:content.DATA=DATA;content.JS=JS
    for f in figures:
        n=f['figure'];f['explanation'][4]=TAKEAWAYS[n];f['live_links']=[dict(label=a,url=b) for a,b in links(n)]
        if n==1:
            f['caption']='Every pair among 179 countries receives a similarity score: 15,931 unique pairs form the complete network. The team-selected P95 cutoff (0.569763) displays 797 high-similarity edges. The boxed bottom rows in Spring and Spectral contain the 12 isolates: countries with no pair above this cutoff. Circular distributes the same nodes around the circle. These positions are drawing coordinates.'
            f['detailed_interpretation']='''Following the professor's advice, we inspect every pair score before filtering. The histogram describes the complete 15,931-pair universe; panel B shows how the cutoff changes connectedness. This makes the filtering decision inspectable.

Our team selects P95 as an exploratory sparse default: the 95th percentile of all pair scores is 0.569763. Scores at or above it retain 797 pairs, approximately the strongest 5%. P95 is neither similarity 0.95, a confidence level nor a percentage of connected countries. The professor did not prescribe this percentile.

All three layouts preserve the 797 edges and India's twelve neighbors. Spring/Spectral position the 167-country connected core and pack the twelve degree-zero countries into a boxed row. Those dots are measured nodes, not a dotted baseline, missing data or a plotting error. They have scores to every other country and may gain links below P95. Circular distributes all 179 countries around its circumference.

P90 retains 1,594 edges and 5 isolates; P92.5 retains 1,195 and 9; P95 retains 797 and 12; P97.5 retains 399 and 22. Stricter filtering removes weaker pairs but can fragment the core. Keeping every country connected requires 11,526 edges. P95 balances this display trade-off without claiming a unique optimum.

The professor mentioned perturbation as one possible selector. Our 30 seeded trials test robustness after choosing P95: Gaussian noise with SD 0.02 in standardized coordinates gives edge Jaccard 0.905-0.941 (mean 0.922). This is neither calibrated measurement error nor a specified published selector. Scaling/feature changes give overlaps 0.801 (median/IQR), 0.460 (ranks) and 0.338-0.512 (one feature omitted). The online method review supplies tables, the continuous matrix and sources. Stable drawings or small-noise checks cannot establish natural country groups.'''
        if n==3:
            f['detailed_interpretation']='''The six non-singleton modularity communities contain 699 internal edges; 98 edges connect different communities. They subdivide one 167-country connected component. The twelve isolates are drawn individually below the overview. A modularity community therefore differs from a disconnected component, a continent or an established country type.

The enlarged group has 15 countries and retains 93 of 105 possible pairs. Afghanistan has 14 internal links and Angola 7. These counts matter because they reveal heterogeneity hidden by a shared group label: one member resembles every other member above the cutoff, while another resembles only half of them. The matrix supplies the exact pair evidence needed to qualify the grouped overview. The counts do not rank development, access quality or national performance.

Read a country row across the columns. Teal marks a score meeting P95; white marks a measured score below it; the gray self-pair diagonal is excluded. Each undirected link is mirrored, so count one triangle. A white cell does not mean no internet connection or no observation. Lowering the cutoff can fill gaps and change the modularity grouping. The correct inference is that aggregation helps organize the graph but must be checked against individual pair relationships.'''
        if n in DETAIL:f['detailed_interpretation']=DETAIL[n]
        if n==22:f['explanation'][1]='Each longitude-sector panel retains all 92 days. The solid colored line is the area-weighted spatial median, the dashed dark line the area-weighted mean, and the shaded ribbon the spatial 10th-90th percentiles. All panels use the same Celsius scale.'
